 out to consider and to resolve  the hiccups the difficulties that arrive  arise from the application of the  protocol everybody agrees there is no  difference there is no division of minds  on that matter where the line is drawn  is when the british government says with  the chest stuck out that they will act  unilaterally that they will legislate  unilaterally and where the british  government very cynically i have to say  um says to unionism or a section of  political unionism that they can have a  veto on progress they can have no such  veto democracy has to rule uh in the end  and the democratic wishes of the people  have to be respected and be very clear  and i i i'm flanked by two uh former  ministers of the executive and the the  first minister elect everybody is very  clear that whatever people's differences  are points of divergence everybody wants  government everybody wants the executive  established so what we needed to hear  from boris johnson and we need to hear  yes  is that the executive must be formed no  ifs no buts no conditionality and no  unionist visa  that we're on the agenda today um  well we expect to see um delivery in  terms of abortion services oct the  cultural package within the next week  that's been given on the issue of legacy  um we have challenged again the british  government's approach to all of this  they are they are absolutely clear that  the amnesty they proposed is  unacceptable it's been roundly rejected  by all shades of opinion uh here on the  island of ireland and indeed condemned  internationally  what their alternative proposal is we  don't know what we do know is that many  many moons ago at stormont house  agreement was found agreement was found  a consensus was found amongst the  political parties here and the the  british government the tories have been  really really very negative and have  helped well we seem to have lost the  line there to hillsborough you were  listening to shin fame president mary  lou macdonald speaking there let's  rejoin her actually here she  is boris johnson and secondly are you  committed to power sharing even if the  prime minister overrides the protocol  will you still be committed to that well  firstly we have not given up on power  sharing or the executive fire from us  parish sharing is the only game in town  working together is the only option  there is no plan b we live here together  we have to work together we have to make  progress together and we are up for that  we will we will work night and day to  make that uh happen  the unfortunate thing is that the  british government now is playing a game  of brinkmanship with the european  institutions  indulging a section of political  unionism which believes that it can hold  a veto and frustrate and hold society uh  to ransom that's what needs to change  our commitment to power sharing our  commitment to working in partnership  will never change and michelle o'neill  uh will say i'm sure for herself how  anxious she is to take office and to  lead for everybody we campaigned on the  basis of establishing functioning  partnership institutions electing a  first minister for all when we said that  we meant that and we believe that in in  the current circumstances so many people  irrespective of how they voted  legitimately expect that they see  functioning government now and and we're  committed to that  sorry sorry there are other people if  the prime minister introduces  legislation overriding the protocol is  that a red line for you  when it comes to power sharing there are  no red lines for us when it comes to  power sharing bar  that power is shared and that others  step up and nominate now let me tell you  there is no circumstances we envisage in  which the protocol can be wished away  spirited way or indeed legislated away  the notion that in the mother of all  parliaments as it boasts  that there would be an attempt to  legislate to break international law is  shocking it's breathtaking 